## Why This Is Asked
Tests low-level bitboard thinking, edge masking, and 128-bit manipulation without loops.

## Key Concepts
- 8x16 bitboard mapping in 128 bits (bit i = row*16 + col).
- Horizontal moves use 1-bit shifts with edge masks (COL0/COL15).
- Vertical moves use 16-bit shifts with TOP/BOTTOM masks.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode close to C for clarity
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
uint128 expandMask128(uint128 m){
  uint128 left  = (m & ~COL0) >> 1;
  uint128 right = (m & ~COL15) << 1;
  uint128 up    = (m & ~TOP) << 16;
  uint128 down  = (m & ~BOTTOM) >> 16;
  return or128(or128(left, right), or128(up, down));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for an irregular 3x5 tile grid embedded in 128 bits?
- What are the performance implications of branching-free bitboard ops on modern CPUs?

## Why This Is Asked
Test ability to count bits with a constant-time sequence of masks and shifts, no loops.

## Key Concepts
- Parallel bit counting
- Fixed masks: 0x5555..., 0x3333..., 0x0F0F...
- No branches

## Code Example
```javascript
function popcount64(x) {
  // x is a BigInt
  x = x - ((x >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333n) + ((x >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n);
  x = (x + (x >> 4n)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  x = x + (x >> 8n);
  x = x + (x >> 16n);
  x = x + (x >> 32n);
  return Number(x & 0x7Fn);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for 128-bit using the same pattern?
- What are the performance implications on 32-bit vs 64-bit targets?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to implement conditional data movement without branching. It demonstrates how to construct a full-word mask from a single flag and apply it consistently across 128-bit halves.

## Key Concepts
- **Branchless conditioning via masks:** Using arithmetic to convert a boolean into an all-0s or all-1s mask
- **128-bit representation:** A 128-bit value split into hi/lo 64-bit halves
- **Portable unsigned arithmetic:** Safe, well-defined behavior across platforms

## Code Example
```c
uint128 swapHalvesIf128(uint128 x, unsigned c) {
    unsigned m = -(unsigned)(c != 0);
    uint64_t hi = (x.hi & ~m) | (x.lo & m);
    uint64_t lo = (x.lo & ~m) | (x.hi & m);
    return (uint128){.hi = hi, .lo = lo};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of 128-bit bitboards, branchless kth-one, and cross-half boundary without loops.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit value as two 64-bit words; popcount and select in constant time
- Branchless kth-one within a 64-bit word
- Correct handling when n is out of range across halves

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
int findNthOne128(uint128 a, unsigned n){
  unsigned loCnt = __builtin_popcountll(a.lo);
  unsigned hiCnt = __builtin_popcountll(a.hi);
  unsigned total = loCnt + hiCnt;
  if (n >= total) return -1;
  if (n < loCnt) return kth1_64(a.lo, n);
  return 64 + kth1_64(a.hi, n - loCnt);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 256-bit values without branches?
- What are trade-offs across compilers for portability vs performance?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests crossing-boundary bit extraction on a 128-bit value with no branching, mirroring real-world crypto/low-level data parsing tasks.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-boundary bit manipulation.
- Mask generation for widths up to 64 without 64-bit overflow.
- Handling 128-bit values via hi/lo halves (no loops).

## Code Example
```javascript
function extractBits128(hi, lo, pos, width) {
  const V = (BigInt(hi) << 64n) | BigInt(lo);
  const mask = width === 64 ? (1n << 64n) - 1n : (1n << BigInt(width)) - 1n;
  return (V >> BigInt(pos)) & mask;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to perform fixed-bit manipulations without loops, using SWAR-style packing to reduce 64 bits to 32 bits in one pass.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR parallel bit extraction
- Fixed-mask chain for bit packing
- No branching for deterministic performance

## Code Example

```c
uint32 compressEvenBits64(uint64 x) {
    x = (x & 0x5555555555555555ULL) | ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
    x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) | ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
    x = (x & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) | ((x >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL);
    x = (x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) | ((x >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL);
    x = (x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) | ((x >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL);
    return (uint32)x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this to extract the odd bits instead of even bits?
- What are the trade-offs vs a loop-based approach on various architectures?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft branchless bit-twiddling in 128-bit space, a common low-level optimization task.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit integer handling via two 64-bit halves
- Trailing-zero count (ctz) tricks without branches
- De Bruijn sequences for fast bit indexing

## Code Example
```c
unsigned countTrailingZeros128(uint128 x) {
  static const unsigned char deBruijn64[64] = {
    0, 1, 2, 53, 3, 7, 54, 27,
    4, 38, 8, 34, 55, 48, 28, 20,
    5, 39, 53, 6, 59, 29, 42, 16,
    9, 35, 60, 50, 45, 27, 21, 17,
    10, 49, 56, 40, 30, 14, 43, 15,
    11, 23, 51, 52, 41, 19, 13, 44,
    12, 26, 58, 36, 31, 37, 29, 22
  };
  unsigned loIdx = ((x.lo & -x.lo) * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a89ull) >> 58;
  unsigned hiIdx = ((x.hi & -x.hi) * 0x03f79d71b4cb0a89ull) >> 58;
  unsigned tzLo = deBruijn64[loIdx];
  unsigned tzHi = deBruijn64[hiIdx];
  unsigned isLoZero = (x.lo == 0);
  return (1 - isLoZero) * tzLo + isLoZero * (64 + tzHi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to support a variable-width 128-bit type with non-power-of-two lanes?
- Compare performance of this approach against a masked binary search approach on modern CPUs.

## Why This Is Asked
Interleaving bits is a practical pattern in spatial indexing (Morton codes) and graphics. This tests branch-free bit twiddling and understanding of bit spreading.

## Key Concepts
- Bit spreading (part1by1)
- Masked shifts and combiners
- Branch-free composition for 64-bit results

## Code Example
```javascript
uint64_t spread32(uint32_t x){
  uint64_t y = x;
  y = (y | (y << 16)) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL;
  y = (y | (y << 8))  & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL;
  y = (y | (y << 4))  & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  y = (y | (y << 2))  & 0x3333333333333333ULL;
  y = (y | (y << 1))  & 0x5555555555555555ULL;
  return y;
}
uint64_t interleave32(uint32_t a, uint32_t b){
  return spread32(a) | (spread32(b) << 1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 64-bit inputs to a 128-bit output?
- What intrinsics would you use on your platform?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about data-parallel bit tricks and boundary-safe shifts without branches.

## Key Concepts

- Byte-wise (SWAR) operations across 128-bit lanes
- Constructing per-byte masks to constrain operations to byte boundaries
- Handling n in 0..7 without loops or conditionals

## Code Example

```cpp
// outline of approach (conceptual; leverages 128-bit value split into bytes)
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint128 perByteRotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
    uint128 M = { 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL };
    uint128 left  = { .hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (8 - n)), .lo = x.lo << n };
    uint128 right = { .hi = (x.hi >> (8 - n)), .lo = (x.lo >> (8 - n)) };
    return { .hi = (left.hi & M.hi) | (right.hi & ~M.hi),
             .lo = (left.lo & M.lo) | (right.lo & ~M.lo) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to 32-bit lanes or to 256-bit types?
- What are the performance implications on modern compilers and ISAs?

## Why This Is Asked

In high-throughput diffing pipelines, diffusion-boosting byte-wise bit reversal can be done with a fixed, branchless sequence, testing mastery of multi-stage bit manipulations.

## Key Concepts

- Parallel bit-twiddling across 8-byte lanes
- Masks: 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F, 0x3333333333333333, 0x5555555555555555
- Branchless, constant-time operations

## Code Example

```javascript
function reverseBitsInBytes64(n) {
  n = ((n >>> 4n) | (n << 4n)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  n = ((n >>> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n) | ((n & 0x3333333333333333n) << 2n);
  n = ((n >>> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n) | ((n & 0x5555555555555555n) << 1n);
  return n;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test edge cases (0, all ones, alternating patterns)?
- How does endianness affect results across platforms?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to craft fixed, branchless bit-twiddling routines across 128‑bit values, a common requirement in high‑performance bitmap or Bloom filter code. It exposes knowledge of SWAR reductions and careful handling of two 64‑bit lanes.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR (SIMD within a register) reductions
- Handling 128-bit as two 64-bit halves
- Fixed-mask sequences and non-branching sums

## Code Example
```c
unsigned popcount128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t a = x.hi;
  a = a - ((a >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  a = (a & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((a >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  a = (a + (a >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  unsigned hi = (a * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;

  uint64_t b = x.lo;
  b = b - ((b >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  b = (b & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((b >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  b = (b + (b >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  unsigned lo = (b * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56;

  return hi + lo;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to a 256-bit type?  - Can you prove correctness with a small formal argument or tests?
